Bonnet Stem Backseat Design:
The bonnet shall include a conical or spherical stem backseat in one of the following forms: a bushing positively secured against coming loose, i.e. not relying on friction;
The bonnet shall include a conical or spherical stem backseat in one of the following forms: an integral surface in the case of an austenitic stainless steel valve;
The bonnet shall include a conical or spherical stem backseat in one of the following forms: an austenitic stainless steel or hardfaced weld deposit that is a minimum of 1.6 mm (0.06 in.) thick.

Bonnet-to-Body Joint Type:
The bonnet-to-body joint shall be a flange and gasket type.
For Class 150 valves, the bonnet-to-body joint shall be one of the following types illustrated in ASME B16.5: flat face;
For Class 150 valves, the bonnet-to-body joint shall be one of the following types illustrated in ASME B16.5: raised face;
For Class 150 valves, the bonnet-to-body joint shall be one of the following types illustrated in ASME B16.5: tongue and groove;
For Class 150 valves, the bonnet-to-body joint shall be one of the following types illustrated in ASME B16.5: spigot and recess (i.e. male and female);
For Class 150 valves, the bonnet-to-body joint shall be one of the following types illustrated in ASME B16.5: ring joint.

Bonnet Flange Gasket Types:
The bonnet flange gasket shall be one of the following: solid metal, corrugated or grooved (profiled) metal gasket with graphite filler;
The bonnet flange gasket shall be one of the following: metal ring joint;
The bonnet flange gasket shall be one of the following: spiral wound metal gasket with filler and a centering/compression ring;
The bonnet flange gasket shall be one of the following: spiral wound metal gasket with filler but without a centering/compression ring, to be used only in ‘tongue and groove’ or ‘spigot and recess’ joints that prevent the gasket from unwinding and from buckling damage.
For Class 150, the following are also acceptable: corrugated metal insert with graphite facings;
For Class 150, the following are also acceptable: when approved by the purchaser, flexible graphite sheet, reinforced with a stainless steel flat, perforated, tanged, or corrugated insert equipped with annular containment rings;
For Class 150, the following are also acceptable: when approved by the purchaser, other suitable facings may be used.

Valve Operation Additions:
If operation by a chain wheel, gearbox or power actuator is to be added to the valve, the purchaser shall specify the following, as applicable: for chainwheel operation, the dimension from the centerline of the valve stem or gear input shaft to the bottom of the chain loop;
spur or bevel gear and the position of gearing handwheel relative to the pipe axis;
electric, hydraulic, pneumatic or other actuator type;
maximum service temperature and pressure differential across the valve disc;
power supply attributes for power actuators.

Purchaser Specifications for Valve Order:
Supplemental requirements of this standard shall be specifically stated in the purchase order.
If no supplemental requirements are to be taken to this standard, the purchase order just needs to refer to API 600 and to specify the items in the following list that are marked with an asterisk (*).
The items listed below without an asterisk are options that may also be specified: valve size *; pressure class *; flanged ends, including flange facing finish (for sizes over DN 600 (NPS 24) specify Series A or B); or welding ends, including bore *; auxiliary connections and openings; valve orientation; additional hard facing of body and/or wedge guides; bonnet gasket and/or bonnet flange facing; tapped openings; wedge gate or double-disc gate; also type of wedge, if required *; lantern ring; chainwheel and chain; gear operation, including type and arrangement, and the design maximum pressure differential across the valve; power operation, including type of power and power unit, and the design maximum pressure differential across the valve; frequency of operation; bypass—specify either flanged or welded bonnet bypass valve; material of the valve shell *; nominal trim material *; any required exceptions to manufacturer’s permissible options (e.g. NACE MR 0103); handwheels; safety shield; chainwheel and safety cables, if furnished as original equipment; alternate stem packing material;

Additional Requirements for Valve Compon:
alternate stem packing material;
bonnet bolting material;
inspection by purchaser;
high pressure closure test;
supplementary examination and testing;
export packaging;
pressure seal bonnet;
materials from ASME B 16.34 Group 3.
